messages:
maria_support: Customer reporting intermittent 502s from our TS SDK when they cancel requests with AbortController. They see repeated retries and eventually a timeout, but CPU stays high and connections pile up. Repro attached.

maria_support: Repro (minimal):
```ts
import fetch from 'undici';
const ac = new AbortController();
fetch('https://api.redwood.example/v1/generate', { signal: ac.signal, method: 'POST', body: '{}' })
  .then(r => r.json())
  .catch(err => console.error('fetch err', err));
setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 50);
```
Behavior: client logs a retry then another retry, then a long hang. Customer uses our TS SDK v1.8.2 which does internal retry/backoff and Node 18.16.0 + undici 4.13.0.

josh_eng: Ack. Do we know if the SDK is creating a fresh AbortController for each retry or reusing the original signal?

maria_support: From their trace it looks like SDK passes the original signal into the retry loop but then calls fetch again without ensuring previous request is fully aborted. They see repeated 'request aborted' errors in logs but sockets remain in CLOSE_WAIT.

evan_sre: Sounds like an undici connection pool keep-alive + aborted stream leak. In older undici versions calling abort doesn't always immediately tear down the socket if the request body has been streamed. Does the customer stream requests?

maria_support: No streaming, tiny payloads. Happens under load though (bursty). They also said upgrading Node to 20 didn't help.

josh_eng: Can you ask for exact versions and a trace? Also need their SDK config: retries, backoff, timeout.

maria_support: They replied: SDK v1.8.2, undici 4.13.0, node 18.16.0, retries: 3 (exponential), per-request timeout: 30s, they set global agent keepAlive true. Stack snippet: 
```
Error: request aborted
    at pipeline (internal_streams.js:...)
    at fetch (undici:...)
```

evan_sre: Ok. Two quick repro hypotheses: (1) SDK-level retry spawns new fetch calls while an aborted request leaves the socket in limbo due to undici pool behavior, (2) Abort signal isn't being cloned/propagated correctly when we wrap it for timeout/backoff. Workarounds: set agent.keepAliveTimeout shorter or disable keepAlive, or upgrade undici -> 5.x where some socket cleanup fixes landed.

josh_eng: Good. Also verify that our SDK doesn't wrap the customer's AbortSignal into a new one and then forget to forward abort events. We should be passing the same signal through each retry attempt or explicitly abort the inner attempts on outer abort.

maria_support: Customer tried disabling keepAlive and the leak stopped (no more accumulating sockets), but they still see extra retries when aborting. They said setting SDK retries=0 avoids the retries but breaks client UX.

evan_sre: For the immediate support answer: advise them to either 1) upgrade undici or 2) set keepAliveTimeout low and set SDK to forward original signal into each fetch attempt. For long term, file an SDK bug to ensure abort propagation across retries and to cancel inflight request before starting next retry.

josh_eng: I'll open a ticket on repo with repro. Also add an integration test covering AbortController + retries to ensure no socket leak. Can someone paste minimal SDK-level fix suggestion?

evan_sre: Suggestion (pseudo):
```ts
// before retry loop: const outerSignal = options.signal;
for (let attempt=0; attempt<=max; attempt++){
  const attemptAc = new AbortController();
  // If outer is aborted, abort inner
  outerSignal?.addEventListener('abort', () => attemptAc.abort());
  const mergedSignal = attemptAc.signal;
  // call fetch(..., { signal: mergedSignal })
  // ensure when we start next attempt we explicitly abort previous attemptAc and await request teardown
}
```
Also ensure to call response.body.cancel() / stream.destroy() on abort for node streams so undici can free the socket.

maria_support: Posted workaround to customer and flagged upgrade path. They confirmed immediate mitigation by disabling keepAlive and lowering retries. :thumbsup:

ci-bot: new issue created -> https://github.com/redwood-inference/ts-sdk/issues/472 (title: "AbortController not propagated/cancelling inflight requests on retry -> socket leak on undici 4.x")

josh_eng: Thanks. Add regression test and mark as high priority for next sprint.

maria_support: Closing loop with customer — will follow up when SDK patch lands.
